State mass, composition, purity, yield, and capacity on compatible bases (@metal-quantities: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metals-and-metallurgical-production, #source "../sources/Metallurgical-Material-and-Heat-Balances-NPTEL.lingua#r_38NWQRDJH3RVM3NNGB71RR1X43", #source "../sources/Nonferrous-Metals-Industries-BREF.lingua#r_19ZD7KKDWVWM20MXVNCQZJMCDB", #source "../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/Quantitative-literacy.lingua#r_4XVN56PQC13JGNFPV0CSGXDCZ2", #metals-and-metallurgical-production "../../../engineering/mining-and-mineral-engineering/operation/Products-specifications-custody-and-refining-interfaces.lingua#r_4WQ464KMKXECT0MF2ASW2H6D4G") { r_5ZX3J8072ECM1VMYKXBD6A2A7M
**Read:** [Materials, energy, and heat balances in metallurgical processes — Satish Ch. Koria](../sources/Metallurgical-Material-and-Heat-Balances-NPTEL.lingua#r_38NWQRDJH3RVM3NNGB71RR1X43), the complete quantity, stoichiometry, and first balance exercises; [Non-ferrous Metals Industries — European Commission BREF](../sources/Nonferrous-Metals-Industries-BREF.lingua#r_19ZD7KKDWVWM20MXVNCQZJMCDB), the full product/feed and measured-performance context for one chosen case; [Quantitative and technical literacy](../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/Quantitative-literacy.lingua#r_4XVN56PQC13JGNFPV0CSGXDCZ2), reuse ratios, units, balances, and useful-output reasoning.

Distinguish mass, amount of substance, mass/mole/volume fraction, particle or phase fraction, wet versus dry mass, and composition reported by element or compound. A percentage is a ratio with a defined basis. Trace quantities require their analytical support and uncertainty, not just additional decimal places.

For an invented 10 t dry feed containing 20% copper by mass, contained copper is 2 t. If 90% of that copper reaches a product containing 99% copper, the product mass is 1.8/0.99 = 20/11 t, about 1.81818 t. Product yield is 2/11, while copper recovery is 0.9. Neither quantity alone supplies the whole constituent or residual account.

Capacity has a material/time basis and depends on grade, recovery, quality, process state, and availability. Nominal charge capacity, throughput of dry feed, output of metallic constituent, and delivered accepted product should not be interchanged. Inventory changes and timing matter when comparing periods.

**Work:** independently check the example with exact fractions and units. Reconstruct the quantities in a full public plant case, identifying missing moisture, assay, stock, or time information. Explain how a change in impurity, phase composition, or product acceptance changes the useful output even if gross feed throughput stays constant. Follow the shared mine-to-customer step next for material identity and the evidence transferred with it.

